wetext-rs is a Rust reimplementation of Wenet's WeTextProcessing, which is a
Python package built on pynini and OpenFST. The FST data this module runs
comes from the Python `wetext` distribution, which is the upstream project's
own build of the same grammars; see `public/dictionaries/wetext-en-tn-NOTICE.txt`.

A copy of the Apache License, Version 2.0, is in `LICENSE-Apache-2.0` beside
this file. Both projects are distributed on an "AS IS" BASIS, WITHOUT
WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Modifications made for tts-ng
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

The copy is upstream's `src/` at version 0.1.2 with the following changes.
The first five came out of the evaluation that decided on a copy rather than a
dependency; the sixth is a correctness fix found after the copy landed, the
seventh is what wiring Chinese and Japanese up needed, the eighth is a defect of
upstream's own that turning `fix_contractions` on exposed, and the ninth replaces
the contraction matcher with the engine upstream's own dependency uses. The tenth
is a notice and not a change. Nothing else about the normalization logic was
touched.

1. `text_normalizer.rs`: added `FstTextNormalizer::from_bytes`, and removed
   `from_file`. `rustfst`'s `SerializableFst::load` takes the whole OpenFST
   binary as a `&[u8]`; upstream's `from_file` only reads a file into a buffer
   first. So the new constructor is that parse with the file read removed, which
   is the half `wasm32-unknown-unknown` cannot do.

2. `normalizer.rs`: the FST cache is no longer lazy. Upstream kept a
   `fst_dir` and read an FST the first time a configuration asked for it;
   here every FST arrives through `Normalizer::from_bytes` under the relative
   name upstream would have joined to that directory. `FstCache` and
   `get_or_load` are gone, upstream's `new(path)` / `with_defaults(path)` /
   free `normalize(path, text)` are gone with them, and `normalize` takes
   `&self` because there is nothing left to fill in. This is what makes the
   module usable where there is no filesystem at all, rather than merely
   compiling without one.

3. `normalizer.rs`: `full_to_half` moved from `postprocess` to `preprocess`.
   Upstream applies it after the TN, which is too late — `should_normalize`
   looks for an ASCII digit, and `２０２２年` has none, so full-width numerals
   reached the tagger unnormalised and the whole TN was skipped, silently.
   Measured on 11 Japanese inputs: 6/11 normalized upstream, 10/11 after the
   move. Japanese and Chinese text is full of full-width digits.

4. `contractions.rs`: the three embedded JSON paths are module-relative
   (`data/...`) rather than crate-relative (`../data/...`), because the file
   moved into `tn/wetext/`. `token_parser.rs`/`contractions.rs` import
   through `super::` for the same reason. No behaviour change.

5. `normalizer.rs`: `should_normalize` regained the `lang` parameter the port
   dropped, so that its digit test applies to every language *except* English —
   which is what the Python reference does. Without it the Rust port skipped the
   whole TN for an English sentence with no digit in it, which is the half of
   English TN that is not about digits: `Dr. Smith` is `doctor Smith` in the
   reference and came out unchanged here. Measured after the fix, the port agreed
   with the reference on 34 of 41 probe inputs. That count was taken **with
   modification 6 not yet made**, and the differences it counted as ties between
   readings of a bare digit string (`123` -> `one hundred and twenty three`
   there, `one two three` here) were in fact modification 6's bug: the two
   readings were not equally costly. The probe was a one-off against
   `pip install wetext==0.1.8` and is not committed, so this note does not claim
   a new number for it — only that the old one should not be read as a limit on
   how well the two agree now. See `README.md`.

6. `text_normalizer.rs`: the one-best path is no longer extracted with
   `rustfst::shortest_path`. That function does not handle **negative arc
   weights**, and these grammars are full of them — upstream ranks competing
   readings with `pynutil.add_weight(..., -0.0001)`, and a composed FST reaches
   `-0.0001` — so it returned paths that were not the cheapest. Measured on the
   composed FST for `cardinal { integer: "123" }`: seven readings exist, the
   cardinal `one hundred and twenty three` costs `0.000000`, `one two three`
   costs `0.000200`, and `shortest_path` returned the latter. Of 18 probe
   sentences through the real pipeline, 7 were read along a more expensive path.
   The copy now runs its own Bellman-Ford relaxation over the composed FST,
   records the arc each state was reached by, picks the final state minimizing
   `dist + final_weight` and walks the arcs back to the start. These grammars
   have no negative cycle (probed: 2-4 relaxation rounds, and the loop ends on a
   round that changes nothing). Same public API, same behaviour for an empty
   compose or an FST with no successful path — the input comes back unchanged.
   Measured on 3000 calls per input, the replacement is not slower than the
   `shortest_path` it replaces (best of five runs: 461 vs 466 us for
   `hello world`, 1273 vs 1289 us for `I have 123 apples.`, 1831 vs 1854 us for
   `Between 100 and 200 people.`). This is our change, not upstream's; upstream
   and `kaldifst` have the same defect.

Additionally, `error.rs` no longer has upstream's
`IoError(#[from] std::io::Error)` variant: it existed for the `std::fs`
constructors that modification 1 and 2 removed, and an unused `From<io::Error>`
is a way for an I/O error to arrive in a module that has no I/O.

7. `normalizer.rs`: `should_normalize` tests digits with
   [`char::is_numeric`](char::is_numeric) rather than `is_ascii_digit`. Upstream's
   line was a port of the Python reference's `re.search(r"\d", text)`, which over
   a Python `str` matches every character in Unicode general category `Nd` — and
   `is_ascii_digit` matches only `0-9`. `char::is_numeric` is `Nd | Nl | No`, a
   superset, so this can only make the normalizer *run* on text the reference
   would have skipped, which returns that text unchanged.

   The port's comment defended the narrower test on the grounds that the
   difference "does not reach English". That was true and beside the point: the
   branch in question is the one English never takes (`lang != En` below it), so
   the difference could only ever reach Chinese and Japanese — and it reached
   them everywhere. Fully half the numerals in real Chinese and Japanese text are
   written full-width (`２０２２年`, `１５．６％`), and `０` is U+FF10. Every one of
   those took the "nothing to normalize here" early return and came out as the
   digits it was written with, silently: dropping to the hand-written reader
   gives the same answer as not having this engine, so no test that only checked
   the output could tell the difference.

   Measured against `pip install wetext==0.1.8`: of 48 Chinese probe inputs the copy agreed on 45 before this change and
   47 after; of 29 Japanese inputs it agreed on 23 before and **29 after**. Every
   remaining disagreement is `1.2.3%`, a malformed number that the reference's
   chained-regex front end and this FST composition fragment differently, and that
   `crates/phonemize/tests/wetext_zh.rs` pins.

   Found by phase 9E, which is when Chinese and Japanese were wired up. Not
   reachable from English, so nothing between 9B and 9E could have noticed.

The port has one more limitation that is **not** ours, recorded in `README.md`:
`1000` is tagged `date { year: "1000" }`, where `ten hundred` and `one thousand`
are a genuine tie at `0.000100`. Modification 6 changed which non-minimal path
was returned; it did not change this, because here both readings *are* the
minimum.

8. `normalizer.rs` and `contractions.rs`: `fix_contractions` now runs, and the
   guard in front of it tests for the apostrophes the tables are keyed with.

   This one is upstream's own defect, fixed here rather than inherited, and it is
   worth separating the two halves:

   - *The guard was one apostrophe short.* Upstream's Python is
     `if config.fix_contractions and "'" in text`, and `contractions.fix` is only
     reached by a straight apostrophe — while the tables the same package builds
     are keyed under both spellings (`contractions_dict.update({k.replace("'",
     "’"): v …})`). So every `’` key is unreachable in the reference, and was
     unreachable here too. The guard is now `contractions::has_apostrophe`, which
     is the question the tables answer; `We’ll` is `we will` and used to be read
     as `We` `L` `L`.
   - *The pattern order was a coin toss.* `PATTERNS` was built by walking a
     `HashMap`, and the tables overlap: `leftovers_dict`'s `'ll` → `" will"` sits
     beside `contractions_dict`'s `we'll` → `"we will"`, and both match the same
     span. Whichever ran first won, and `HashMap`'s iteration order is seeded per
     instance — measured over six runs of the test binary, `We'll go` came back
     `we will go` three times and `We will go` three times. Upstream does not have
     this: `contractions.fix` goes through `TextSearch`, which takes the longest
     key. The copy now sorts the patterns longest-key-first, which restores that
     and makes the reading deterministic.

   - *The convention keys were case-blind, and ate prose.* Seven of the slang
     table's forty-eight entries are writing conventions rather than words (`b4`,
     `bc`, `kk`, `r `, `rn`, `u`, `ur`), and matching them case-insensitively —
     which is what the whole table gets — turns `It's 500 BC` into `it is five
     hundred because`, `U.S.` into `you.S.` and `RN` into `right now`. Upstream
     does the same (`contractions.fix("It's 500 BC")` is `It's 500 BECAUSE`), so
     the copy now matches those seven exactly and everything else
     case-insensitively: the conventions are only ever written in lower case and
     what they collide with is only ever written in upper case. The predicate is
     `contractions::is_a_convention_rather_than_a_word` — "two characters or fewer,
     or containing a digit" — so that a table bump adding `gr8` or `2day` is
     covered without anyone remembering to.

   None of the three is a change to the tables, and none is reachable while
   `fix_contractions` is off — which is upstream's default, and was this crate's
   until the reading was reported as a bug. `crate::tn::engine` turns it on for
   English only, and all three are tested in `contractions.rs` and in
   `tests/wetext_en.rs`.

9. `contractions.rs`: the 412 `Regex` objects became one `AhoCorasick` automaton.

   `fix_contractions` was one `(?i)\b…\b` regex per key, applied in a loop over the
   mutating string. The keys are literals and the loop exists to emulate
   `TextSearch`, which is what the Python `contractions` package (and therefore the
   Python `wetext`) matches them with — so the port was emulating a multi-string
   automaton with regexes, one per key. It now uses `aho-corasick`, which is that
   automaton, and which `regex` already linked.

   **The build is what forced it, not the scan.** Measured over the shipped 412
   keys: 412 patterns compiled on the first apostrophe-bearing sentence is 56 ms and
   6.7 MB of resident heap, against 0.73 ms and 89 KB for the automaton — and the
   first call is the first sentence of an article, not a moment anybody chose. The
   per-sentence scan barely moves (30 µs → 2.2 µs for a sentence, 80 µs → 14 µs for
   a paragraph, against 500–750 ms of synthesis).

   The automaton is a `MatchKind::Standard` one, and it is *not* what picks the key:
   it reports every match and the scan filters, because the boundaries and the case
   rule are not in the literals. Two measurements decide the shape, both of them
   taken rather than assumed: `Standard` resolves two keys matching at the same
   position by taking the one that ends *first* (`o'` over `o'clock`), so the scan
   applies leftmost-then-longest itself; and a match the tables reject hides the
   shorter ones inside or at the start of it — `ayou’d` is `you’d` over `’d`, and
   `o'clocks` is `o'clock` over `o'` — so the scan enumerates every match instead of
   filtering a leftmost-longest answer. Enumerating all of them to the end of the
   text costs 174 µs on a paragraph, which is more than the 412-regex loop this
   replaces (80 µs); the early exit in `Tables::expand` — sound because the iterator
   yields matches in end-offset order and no key is longer than `max_key_len` — is
   what makes one automaton sufficient, at 1.6 µs a sentence and 16 µs a paragraph
   against 0.92 µs and 8.6 µs for the two-automaton shape that preceded it. An
   earlier revision of this modification had that second automaton; the early exit
   removed it.

   The wrong shapes and the hidden matches were found by diffing the new engine
   against the old one over every key in eleven carriers plus prose
   (`the_engine_swap_changes_nothing_over_these_inputs`), not by reading.

   Two consequences worth naming. The boundaries and the case rule are now this
   module's own predicates rather than the regex engine's, so `is_word` asks
   `char::is_alphanumeric()` where `\b` asked Unicode's `\w` — the same for every
   ASCII character, and different for combining marks and join controls on one side
   and `No`/`Nl` (`①`, `Ⅷ`, `²³¹¼½¾`) on the other; the test
   `is_word_agrees_with_the_regex_word_class_over_these_code_points` pins both
   directions and the counts. And ASCII case folding replaces Unicode's, so `ſhe's` no longer
   expands — which is what upstream does, making that the one input class where the
   two engines disagree and the new one is closer to the reference.

   `warm_up` is the entry point `crate::tn::engine::english` calls, so the build
   happens in `finish_loading` beside the FST parses rather than in a sentence.

10. `data/contractions-NOTICE.txt`: added, for the three JSON tables beside it.

    Upstream `wetext-rs` embeds `contractions` 0.1.73's `contractions_dict.json`,
    `leftovers_dict.json` and `slang_dict.json` under its own Apache-2.0 LICENSE
    and attributes none of them, so this copy did not either. They are MIT —
    Copyright (c) 2021 Pascal van Kooten — and the tables are not a detail of
    `wetext-rs`'s own licence: the Python `wetext` distribution declares
    `contractions` as a runtime dependency, and its `normalize` calls
    `contractions.fix(text)` before any grammar of its own runs.

    So the notice is added rather than the files being changed: nothing about the
    normalizer's behaviour depends on it, and the table bytes are still the MIT
    package's, unmodified. `data/contractions-NOTICE.txt` carries the licence text,
    the hashes that make "unmodified" checkable, and the provenance — including the
    note that the missing attribution came from upstream rather than from a decision
    here.

    **It is the only non-Apache licence in this directory**, which is why it is a
    file of its own rather than a paragraph in this one: `wetext-rs` and
    WeTextProcessing are Apache-2.0 and cover the FSTs and the ported code, and this
    covers data neither of them wrote.
